<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<h2>Why Speed Alone Doesn't Decide the Position</h2>
<p>None of this means speed has replaced arm talent as the top priority at quarterback. Accuracy, anticipation, and processing speed under pressure remain the primary factors that separate franchise quarterbacks from athletic ones who never develop as passers. What's changed is the floor — mobility is now treated as a real asset rather than a novelty, and prospects who combine passing fundamentals with legitimate athleticism carry a real evaluation advantage over pure pocket passers with similar accuracy grades.</p>
<p>That shift has downstream effects beyond the draft. Defenses have to prepare differently for a mobile quarterback, spying or accounting for scrambles in ways that change coverage math on every snap. A quarterback who can consistently threaten with his legs adds an extra layer of unpredictability that's difficult to defend, even when he isn't the most explosive athlete on the field.</p>
<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<h2>What to Watch For in Training Camp</h2>
<p>Camp is where the gap between combine speed and functional football speed gets tested. A fast 40 time in shorts doesn't guarantee a quarterback can consistently create explosive plays with his legs behind a live offensive line, against a real pass rush, in read-option or scramble situations. Coaches and evaluators will be watching how the more athletic quarterbacks in this class translate straight-line speed into functional pocket mobility and designed-run production once camp begins.</p>
<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What was the average quarterback 40-yard dash time at the 2026 NFL Combine?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Quarterbacks averaged 4.60 seconds in the 40-yard dash at the 2026 NFL Combine, the second-fastest average for the position in combine history.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is considered a fast 40 time for an NFL quarterback?</div>
<div class="faq-a">At the NFL combine, a 40-yard dash under 4.7 seconds is considered fast for a quarterback, given the position's historical average sits closer to 4.8 to 4.9 seconds. A time under 4.6 seconds, like the 2026 combine average, is exceptional for the position.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Why are NFL quarterbacks getting faster?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Modern NFL offenses increasingly use quarterbacks as both passers and runners, incorporating designed runs, zone-read concepts, and extended plays outside the pocket. As offensive schemes have evolved to value mobility, college and high school quarterback development has followed, producing more athletic prospects at the position than in previous eras.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Does a fast 40 time matter for quarterback success in the NFL?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Speed alone does not determine quarterback success — accuracy, decision-making, and processing speed remain the primary factors NFL teams evaluate. However, mobility adds a meaningful dimension by extending plays, creating rushing production, and forcing defenses to account for the quarterback as a running threat, which can elevate an offense even when passing fundamentals are the deciding factor in evaluation.</div>
</div>
